<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (For decades, the organizational transformation literature has wrestled with the same question: Why do well-designed strategies so often fail during execution? The answers are typically labeled as "resistance to change," "organizational culture," or "poor leadership." While these labels may describe the symptoms, they do not explain the underlying cause. The more fundamental question is: What is the organizational system producing in the brains of the people who work within it?)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (The concept of a Brain-Compatible Organization places this question at the center. Whether an organization is truly brain-compatible is not determined by aspirational statements or good intentions, but by five structural dimensions that can be systematically assessed. Each dimension is not an isolated best practice; rather, it is part of an integrated system in which the value of each component depends on the presence and effectiveness of the others.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
