1251 0 obj
<<
/Title (The fundamental reason is that organizations often assume that redesigning processes will naturally lead to changes in human behavior. In reality, what changes is not the process itself, but the decisions people make within it, their habits, learning patterns, collaborative behaviors, and responses to change. Behavior is not merely the outcome of management practices; it is rooted in the biological and cognitive principles that govern the human brain.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 919 0 R
/Prev 1072 0 R
/Next 1359 0 R
>>
endobj
1252 0 obj
<<
/Title (In the years ahead, the defining difference between organizations will not be the technologies they deploy, but how effectively they enable people to think, decide, learn, collaborate, and behave while working alongside those technologies.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 919 0 R
/Prev 1360 0 R
/Next 1073 0 R
>>
endobj
1253 0 obj
<<
/Title (For more than 125 years, the field of management has focused on three fundamental dimensions of organizations: structure, processes, and strategy. Over time, hundreds of frameworks, methodologies, and entire consulting industries have been built around these three pillars.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1074 0 R
/Next 1361 0 R
>>
endobj
1254 0 obj
<<
/Title (Behavior, however, does not emerge spontaneously. It is shaped by the brain's threat and reward systems, habit loops, social networks, identity structures, and cognitive capacity. These systems are, in turn, influenced by the architecture of the organization itself. This scientific understanding marks the starting point of a new paradigm: organizational behavior must be intentionally designed, not simply expected to emerge.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1074 0 R
/Prev 1362 0 R
>>
endobj
